                        * * * *  USERDATA v1.0  * * * *

   This is my first release of a pfile for the Cnet system and therefore the
code could probably be tightened considerably, but as far as I can tell you
will not find any 'BUGS'.

   Userdata is a program which lists the user's access to different areas
of the BBS including the general access to subboards. All information is
completely configurable by the SysOp.

   You may wish to activate this file in either/both of two places in your BBS.

   If you wish it to be part of the sys.welcome file(s) then add it as:

                       \#0pfiles:userdata.rx\

   If you wish it to be part of the Main menu then add it to bbsmenu under the
MAIN section as:

           DA,         |\#0pfiles:userdata.rx\

   I use it in both places, giving my users the opportunity to view their
access status at any time. I have found this file will lead to users asking
for a higher access (ie: subscribers, etc. meaning $$$) to get at areas they
don't have access to.

DESCRIPTION OF CONFIGURING THE PFILE:

   Every section of Userdata is changable to meet your system needs. Since I
 use MicroEmacs, I can list line numbers in this description for you to use
 for your changes:

Lines 16-29

  These lines are the names of the different general subboards. (Note: these
are NOT the individual subboards you may be operating, but general areas -
meaning that IBM subboards means subboards designated for IBM users - or Public
Forum Boards means everyone can enter them, etc.)

  The two letter variable (sa, sb, sc, etc.) represents a subboard
     in sa the (s) means subboard and the (a) indicates Public)

   Just change the name to what you want! I have then set up as they are on my
BBS!

Lines 33-46

   These are the access levels which determine the Yes/No access responses in
the areas of pfiles/gfiles/email/etc. You can change the levels of access by
simply raising or lowering the numbers for each area

   if ac>0 then  ---  could be set as:  if ac=2 then 
       
(if access is greater than 0 then)


Lines 55-91

These are the flags for each general subboard area

             y = full access
             n = no access
             p = limited/restricted access

each line in this area represents a user access level

each 2-letter variable starting with n flags a general subboard: the second
letter specifies which subboard

            example flag na = subboard a (sa = Public subboards)

You will see: (example)

if ac=0 then na=n; nb=y; nc=p; nd=n; ne=n; (and so on)

this means if the user access is 0 then subboard a is (n) no access;
subboard b is (y) full access; subboard c is (p) Ltd. access; and 
subboards d & e are (n) no access.

   You may change any of these variables to meet your needs.

Since I have not seen many BBSes with more than 14 'general' subboard areas
I limited that at this time. If more are wanted, I will expand it in future
versions.

